@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Setting Up End-to-End CI/CD Pipeline";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">A well-structured CI/CD pipeline automates software build, testing, and deployment, ensuring fast and reliable delivery. Setting up an end-to-end CI/CD pipeline in Azure DevOps improves efficiency and reduces manual errors.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What is CI/CD?</h2>
        <p>Continuous Integration (CI) and Continuous Deployment (CD) streamline the development process by automating software delivery. The key benefits include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Faster Releases:</strong> Automates build, test, and deployment processes.</li>
            <li><strong>Improved Code Quality:</strong> Catches bugs early with automated testing.</li>
            <li><strong>Consistent Deployments:</strong> Eliminates manual deployment errors.</li>
            <li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Deploys changes across multiple environments seamlessly.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="text-center mt-4">
        <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/ci-cd-pipeline.png" alt="CI/CD Workflow" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
        <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>CI/CD Pipeline Flow</small></p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Steps to Set Up a CI/CD Pipeline in Azure DevOps</h2>
        <p>Follow these key steps to set up an end-to-end CI/CD pipeline:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Set Up a Source Code Repository:</strong> Use Azure Repos or GitHub to store and manage code.</li>
            <li><strong>Define a Build Pipeline:</strong> Automate compilation, unit tests, and packaging.</li>
            <li><strong>Configure Automated Testing:</strong> Include unit, integration, and security tests in the pipeline.</li>
            <li><strong>Set Up Deployment Stages:</strong> Create separate environments for development, staging, and production.</li>
            <li><strong>Implement Approval Gates:</strong> Require manual approvals for production releases.</li>
            <li><strong>Monitor Deployments:</strong> Use Azure Monitor and Application Insights for tracking performance.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Example YAML for a CI/CD Pipeline</h3>
        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded">
stages:
- stage: Build
  jobs:
  - job: BuildJob
    steps:
    - script: echo "Building the application"
    - task: DotNetCoreCLI@2
      inputs:
        command: 'build'
        projects: '**/*.csproj'

- stage: Deploy
  jobs:
  - job: DeployJob
    steps:
    - script: echo "Deploying application"
    - task: AzureWebApp@1
      inputs:
        azureSubscription: 'MySubscription'
        appName: 'MyApp'
        package: '$(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)/drop.zip'
        </pre>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Best Practices for CI/CD Pipelines</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Automate Everything:</strong> From code integration to testing and deployment.</li>
            <li><strong>Use Infrastructure as Code (IaC):</strong> Define infrastructure with Terraform or ARM templates.</li>
            <li><strong>Enable Security Scanning:</strong> Use SonarQube, WhiteSource, or Snyk for vulnerability detection.</li>
            <li><strong>Optimize Pipeline Speed:</strong> Use caching, parallel jobs, and incremental builds.</li>
            <li><strong>Implement Rollback Strategies:</strong> Prepare automated rollback mechanisms for failed deployments.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <p>By setting up an efficient CI/CD pipeline, teams can achieve faster development cycles, reduce downtime, and improve application quality. Implementing DevOps best practices ensures continuous delivery of high-quality software.</p>
    </div>

